  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "The moment his grandmother steps out the door, Max stops Nastya at the bar to ask what his grandfather just ordered. Learning that the old man asked for roast meat and two hundred grams of strong alcohol, Max cancels the order and serves him steamed diet food instead.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Погоди!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "А что мой дед заказал?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Мяса жареного какого-нибудь.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Выпить что-нибудь покрепче, грамм двести.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "И компот.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ясно. Мясо не неси, только компот.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Хорошо, Макс.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Вот твой обед, дед.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Вот это сервис!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "А где же моё мясо?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Тебе нельзя мясо, дед.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Ну ладно, внучок.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Погоди!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Wait!' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'to wait a little' before landing on 'to wait a little'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "А что мой дед заказал?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And what did my grandpa order?' The meaning hinges on 'and' working together with 'ordered' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Мяса жареного какого-нибудь.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Some kind of roast meat.' Nastya brings out the contrast between 'meat' and 'roasted' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Выпить что-нибудь покрепче, грамм двести.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Something stronger to drink, about two hundred grams.' Nastya anchors the line on 'to drink', while 'two hundred' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "И компот.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And stewed fruit compote.' The grammar packs 'and' and 'stewed fruit drink' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ясно. Мясо не неси, только компот.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Got it. Do not bring the meat, only the compote.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'clear' and finishing on 'stewed fruit drink'. Nastya answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Хорошо, Макс.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Alright, Max.' In Russian, the idea of 'well' flows naturally into 'Max' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Вот твой обед, дед.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Here is your lunch, grandpa.' The grammar packs 'here' and 'grandfather' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Вот это сервис!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Now that is service!' Notice how Russian expresses 'here' and 'service' with crisp inflection rather than helper words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "А где же моё мясо?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And where is my meat?' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'and' before landing on 'meat'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Тебе нельзя мясо, дед.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You are not allowed meat, grandpa.' The meaning hinges on 'to you' working together with 'grandfather' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Ну ладно, внучок.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Well alright, grandson.' the guest anchors the line on 'come on', while 'grandson' completes the thought. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
